<%#- ESCAPING: almost all of this is literal CSS. <%% emits runtime ERB into
     the generated view — here only the comment; a bare <% is the one
     generation-time branch, at the bottom. -%>
<%%# The app's half of the transport contract.

    Every reactivity in this stack is server-side, so every gesture is a round
    trip. The hibiki client stamps what it knows about that trip on the island
    root and on the control that fired — data-hibiki-busy, aria-busy and
    data-hibiki-state — and these rules are the only thing that turns those
    attributes into something a user can see. They are the one part of the
    data-hibiki protocol app code may touch, and only by READING it: no helper
    emits these attributes, and nothing here should ever write one.

    Nothing below hides or replaces content, and no view gained an
    `if loading` branch. During a round trip the list on screen is STALE, not
    absent — the server-rendered first paint is real content, and every later
    update swaps valid content for newer valid content. So the idiom is to
    annotate the stale thing, not to swap it for a skeleton.

    A <style> element rather than a stylesheet: it needs no build step and no
    guess about which asset pipeline this app uses. Move it into
    app/assets/stylesheets if you turn on a Content-Security-Policy that
    forbids inline styles. %>
<style>
  @keyframes hbk-slide { from { transform: translateX(-100%) } to { transform: translateX(340%) } }

  /* THE ISLAND is busy. The island root is the only element that ever carries
     data-hibiki-state, so requiring both attributes is how a rule says "this
     island" rather than "some control inside it" — which matters, because the
     firing control carries data-hibiki-busy too.

     visibility, not display: an indicator that appears must not reflow the
     sentence or the list it sits above. It reserves its own space. */
  .hbk-island-busy { visibility: hidden }
  [data-hibiki-state][data-hibiki-busy] .hbk-island-busy { visibility: visible }

  /* The bar above the list. Non-blocking on purpose: the list underneath it
     is still the real list, still scrollable, still clickable.

     A faint track under the moving segment, so the bar reads as present
     even at the moments the segment is off at one end — and both tinted
     from currentColor with color-mix rather than `opacity`, which would
     stack and leave the segment at 4% of what it should be. */
  .hbk-bar {
    height: 3px; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 3px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 14%, transparent);
  }
  .hbk-bar::after {
    content: ""; display: block; height: 100%; width: 30%;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 55%, transparent);
    transform: translateX(-100%);
  }
  [data-hibiki-state][data-hibiki-busy] .hbk-bar::after {
    animation: hbk-slide 1.1s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* THIS CONTROL is busy. :not([data-hibiki-state]) excludes the island root,
     so one row's Save spins that row's button and no other. A descendant
     selector rather than a child one because the control that fires a submit
     is the FORM, and the spinner lives inside its button. */
  .hbk-control-busy { display: none }
  [data-hibiki-busy]:not([data-hibiki-state]) .hbk-control-busy { display: inline-block }

  /* Buttons and links dim while their own trip is in flight — the answer to
     "did my click register?", free at every site because the client stamps
     the control it fired from. Dimmed, never disabled: toggling `disabled`
     mid-gesture drops keyboard focus, and the label stays put so nothing
     reflows under the pointer.

     Text inputs are deliberately NOT in this list. A debounced search fires
     while the user is still typing, and dimming the field they are typing
     into reads as a fault. The island-level indicator covers that case. */
  button[data-hibiki-busy], a[data-hibiki-busy] { opacity: .55; cursor: progress }

  /* Connection state is not busy-ness: the content is real and frozen, and
     the honest thing is to say so rather than to spin. `connecting` gets no
     rule at all — the client queues actions until the subscription confirms,
     so that window is just a slow round trip now, and the busy machine
     already covers it. */
  .hbk-offline, .hbk-stalled { display: none }
  [data-hibiki-state="offline"] .hbk-offline,
  [data-hibiki-state="stalled"] .hbk-stalled { display: inline-block }
<% unless css? -%>

  /* --css=none, so the spinner's LOOK has nowhere else to come from. The
     styled variants get theirs from a class token instead.

     Note what is NOT here: `display`. The rules above own that — a control
     spinner is display:none until its control is busy — and declaring it
     again at equal specificity, further down the file, would win and leave
     every spinner permanently visible. The island's own dot needs it, since
     a bare span is inline and would ignore the width, so it gets it from a
     two-class selector that nothing else matches. */
  @keyframes hbk-rotate { to { transform: rotate(360deg) } }
  .hbk-spinner {
    box-sizing: border-box; width: .8em; height: .8em;
    border: 2px solid currentColor; border-right-color: transparent;
    border-radius: 50%; vertical-align: -.1em;
  }
  .hbk-island-busy.hbk-spinner { display: inline-block }
  [data-hibiki-busy] .hbk-spinner { animation: hbk-rotate .7s linear infinite }
<% end -%>
</style>
